---
/**
 * Fender Jaguar dating guide. FOURTH OF THE MODEL-AXIS SPOKES.
 *
 * Sibling of fender-stratocaster-dating-guide.astro,
 * fender-telecaster-dating-guide.astro and fender-jazzmaster-dating-guide.astro.
 * Built on joe/jaguar-dating-spoke, stacked on joe/jazzmaster-dating-spoke,
 * because every model page edits the hub's "Go Deeper" chips and the sitemap
 * list and would otherwise conflict. Joe is holding the model set to go live
 * together.
 *
 * WHAT MAKES THE JAGUAR PAGE DIFFERENT. Do not clone the structure of the other
 * three. This model inverts the central move of all of them:
 *   - ON EVERY OTHER MODEL PAGE THE DECAL IS A PRIMARY DATING TOOL. ON A JAGUAR
 *     IT IS NOT. The gold transition logo ran from the 1962 launch through about
 *     mid-1968, spanning the pre-CBS years AND the early CBS years, so the logo
 *     alone cannot tell you which side of CBS a Jaguar sits on. That is the
 *     page's hook and it is why the logo is deliberately LEFT OUT of the five
 *     fastest checks and given its own "why this won't help" section instead.
 *   - THE JAGUAR NEVER WORE A SPAGHETTI LOGO. It arrived after that era ended.
 *     A listing claiming one is describing the headstock wrong or showing a
 *     replaced decal.
 *   - The PATENT BLOCK does what the logo cannot, and its count DROPS from 5 or
 *     6 to 2 at the 1968 CBS decal, which is counter-intuitive and is the single
 *     best test against a counterfeit decal.
 *   - The BOUND-DOT WINDOW: binding arrives about September 1965, blocks not
 *     until mid-1966, so bound board + pearloid dots is a correct factory build.
 *   - FULLERPLAST is the cleanest refinish tell on any 1963 or later Jaguar, and
 *     it gets its own section. Note the misconception the guide corrects:
 *     Fullerplast is CLEAR. The yellow amber is the aniline dye stain UNDER it,
 *     not a colored sealer and not an undercoat.
 *   - The BUZZ STOP drills two permanent holes in the original tremolo plate.
 *     That is an authentication check no other model page needs.
 *   - 24 inch scale, the mute, and the dual circuit are all Jaguar ground.
 *
 * JOE'S REVIEW RULES, carried over from the three sibling pages:
 *   - Features give a WINDOW, often a wider one than the serial. They
 *     corroborate the serial rather than beating it. Only the neck heel date
 *     and the body date give a month.
 *   - The serial belongs in the second pass, read as one vote among several.
 *   - Never call a feature "model-specific" without checking it.
 *   - Never say "bare wood" about a body date. Say the date is written
 *     directly on the body. (NOTE: "bare wood" IS correct and is KEPT in the
 *     Fullerplast and nail-hole sections, where it is the actual authentication
 *     point, matching how the 1962/1963 Strat and 1952 P-Bass guides use it.)
 *   - Keep a photo caption on the one thing the photo is for.
 *   - Open the photo before repeating any color or layout claim about it.
 *
 * LOGO COLOR. Not an issue on this model and do not import the argument from the
 * sibling pages. The Jaguar's only pre-CBS logo IS the gold transition logo, so
 * there is no spaghetti-era silver-or-gold question here at all. (For reference:
 * Joe ruled the Stratocaster and Jazzmaster spaghetti logos GOLD; the Telecaster
 * splits silver on the standard model from gold on the Custom.)
 *
 * NECK HEEL MODEL CODE, cross-checked against fender-neck-dates.astro, whose
 * code table is Joe-supplied and Joe-corrected (2026-07-20): the Jaguar is
 * code 1 from 1962 to 1965, and it becomes 15 from 1966 in the end-of-1965
 * renumber. Joe's Jaguar guide gives the same code 1 and the example 1FEB63B.
 * So unlike the Telecaster (code 3, which never moved) the Jaguar code DID move,
 * which makes it a real dating tool on this model.
 *
 * CONTRADICTION IN THE SOURCE, flagged to Joe, NOT propagated: the Jaguar guide
 * says in its pre-CBS section that neck dates were "rubber-stamped on the heel,
 * which is what Fender had moved to by the time the Jaguar launched" and that a
 * penciled name on a Jaguar heel "is a reason to look harder", but its Neck Date
 * Stamps section then says "Pre-CBS necks usually have a penciled date written
 * by the shaper". Those cannot both be true for a model that launched in 1962.
 * The neck-dates spoke puts the ink stamp at "from about 1962", which agrees
 * with the first version, so this page says Jaguars are stamped and does not
 * repeat the penciled-pre-CBS sentence.
 *
 * Content facts trace to Joe's own live pages: primarily the Jaguar
 * authentication guide (vintage-fender-jaguar-guide), plus
 * fender-neck-dates.astro (the model code table), fender-pot-codes.astro,
 * fender-body-dates.astro, fender-physical-features.astro and the custom color
 * authentication guide (the neck pocket photo). General Fender context from
 * VG_FACTS.md: VG-0066 to VG-0068 (serial eras, ranges NOT asserted),
 * VG-0071 (CBS-era tells), VG-0072 (pre-CBS definition), VG-0073 / VG-0074
 * (custom color and refinish value).
 *
 * NOT YET IN VG_FACTS, flagged for a joes-vintage-databases PR: the Jaguar
 * patent-count progression and the counter-intuitive 1968 drop to 2; the
 * Bound-Dot window dates; the black-to-gray pickup bottom change at mid-1964;
 * the flat-to-staggered pole piece transition; the mute discontinuation around
 * 1964; the Buzz Stop drilled-plate tell; and the Jaguar model code 1 to 15
 * renumber. There are NO Jaguar-specific entries in VG_FACTS today.
 *
 * NO YEAR-TO-SERIAL-NUMBER TABLE, same as the three sibling pages. Fender
 * serials are not model-specific, so the charts live once, on the hub.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LicensedImage from "../components/primitives/LicensedImage.astro";
import { resolveImage, assetSrc, ogCardSize } from "../lib/images";
