const HUB_HREF = HUB_PATH;
const OG = `images/blog/${JG}/1966-fender-jaguar-olympic-white-1-scaled.jpg`;

// ---- Data ----
const yearRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  [
    "1962",
    "Production starts in the late spring at the top of the Fender price list. Slab Brazilian rosewood board with clay dots to about July, then the veneer board. Four ply nitrate tortoise pickguard, gold transition logo with a three patent block, black bottom pickups with flat pole pieces, single line Kluson tuners, and the hinged string mute behind the bridge. Serials run in the 80000 and 90000 ranges shared with other models, moving to the L prefix late in the year.",
    "A slab board with clay dots, and a mute still bolted behind the bridge.",
  ],
  [
    "1963",
    "The specification settles into its pre-CBS shape and stays there. Veneer Brazilian rosewood, clay dots, four ply nitrate guard, single line Klusons. The patent block gains a fourth number. Fullerplast sealer is in use under the finish from around now, which becomes the cleanest refinish test on the guitar. The mute is dropped from most production during the year.",
    "Nothing visual changed from late 1962. Go to the heel stamp and the pots.",
  ],
  [
    "1964",
    "The transition starts before the ownership change does. Pearloid dots begin appearing late in the year with clay running through most of production. The pickup bottoms move from black to gray around the middle of the year, and the Kluson stamp goes from one line to two late in the year. The four ply nitrate guard continues.",
    "Pull a pickup. Black bottom is early 1964 or before, gray is late 1964 or after.",
  ],
  [
    "1965",
    "The year collectors argue about hardest. CBS completes the purchase on January 5 but the floor keeps building from existing parts, so an early 1965 with clay dots and a nitrate guard is a pre-CBS guitar in everything but the calendar. Across the year the guard goes to three ply, white fingerboard binding arrives around September, and the L plate gives way to the big F plate late in the year.",
    "Binding on the board. It arrives around September and nothing before it has any.",
  ],
  [
    "1966",
    "Indian rosewood replaces Brazilian. Early 1966 production is mostly the bound board with pearloid dots still fitted, and the pearloid block inlays arrive around the middle of the year, with a few weeks where both leave the line. From mid-1966 the bound and blocked board is the standard build to the end.",
    "Bound board with dots is early 1966. Bound board with blocks is mid-1966 or later.",
  ],
  [
    "1967",
    "Build quality is still high. The maple cap fingerboard appears as an option, a separate maple board glued to a maple neck with a visible seam at the headstock rather than the one piece neck of the 1950s. Late in the year the F tuners replace the Klusons. Nitro starts giving way to a thin polyester on some production.",
    "A seam across the headstock face means a maple cap, and that is 1967 or later.",
  ],
  [
    "1968",
    "Two separate changes that did not land together, which is the source of most 1968 confusion. Polyester rolls in across the first half of the year, and the black CBS logo replaces the gold transition decal around the middle of the year. The factory kept using up old gold decal stock afterward, so a late 1968 with a polyester finish and a gold transition logo is a legitimate factory build.",
    "A thick glassy finish under an old gold decal is a real combination, not a fake.",
  ],
  [
    "1969 to 1972",
    "The cost cutting shows. Thick polyester throughout, the black logo with its two patent block, three ply guards often in pearl rather than tortoise, and less consistent fret and nut work. Maple cap and bound rosewood run alongside each other.",
    "Nothing new to separate these years. The heel stamp and the pot codes do the work.",
  ],
  [
    "1973 to 1975",
    "The last of the run. Maple boards frequently carry black block inlays with black binding rather than pearloid blocks with white, which is a factory 1970s look rather than a replacement neck. Parts mixes get more common as the model wound down. The Jaguar was discontinued in 1975.",
    "Black blocks in black binding on a maple board. Correct here, wrong before it.",
  ],
];

// The checks that place a Jaguar in an era without tools. These give a WINDOW.
// THE LOGO IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. On this model it does not date the guitar,
// which is the point of the #headstock section. Do not "helpfully" add it here.
const fastRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["The inlays", "Clay dots to late 1964, pearloid dots to mid-1966, pearloid blocks after", "Clay is matte and ages tan or cream. Pearloid is bright and catches the light. Blocks are the CBS neck."],
  ["The fingerboard binding", "None to about September 1965, white binding after", "A single white line down each edge of the board. The cleanest CBS marker on the guitar, and readable from a photo."],
  ["The pickguard", "Four ply nitrate to mid-1965, three ply after", "Hold the edge to a strong light and count the layers. The nitrate one also shrinks, cracks, and smells of vinegar."],
  ["The pickup bottoms", "Black Forbon to about mid-1964, gray after", "The one check worth a screwdriver early, because black bottoms carry a real premium on a 1962 to early 1964 guitar."],
  ["The tuners", "Single line Kluson to late 1964, double line to late 1967, F tuners after", "Read the stamp on the back plate. One line of text, two stacked lines, or a large stylized F."],
];

// Jaguar neck-heel model code. Cross-checked against fender-neck-dates.astro.
const codeRows: [string, string][] = [
  ["1", "Jaguar, 1962 to 1965, in the rubber-stamped heel codes (for example 1FEB63B)"],
  ["15", "Jaguar from 1966, after Fender renumbered the model codes at the end of 1965"],
];

// Patent block by era. This is what the logo cannot do on a Jaguar.
const patentRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["1962", "3 patents", "2,960,900 offset body, 2,972,923 vibrato, and 2,741,146. PAT. PEND. text may sit alongside the numbers on the earliest examples."],
  ["1963 to 1964", "4 patents", "The 1962 three plus 3,143,028. Already a bigger block than a same-year Stratocaster decal. PAT. PEND. often lingers on early 1963 as old stock was used up."],
  ["1965 to 1967", "5 to 6 patents", "The earlier numbers plus DES 186,826 and others. The block becomes a thick multi-line paragraph, the largest patent text a Jaguar headstock ever carried."],
  ["1968 to 1975", "2 patents", "2,972,923 and 3,143,028 only. The count DROPS at the black CBS decal, which is the opposite of what most people expect."],
];

// Serial plate eras. Named, NOT charted. Charts live on the hub.
const plateRows: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["Plain chrome four-bolt plate", "1962", "Five digits in the 80000 and 90000 ranges, no letter, shared with Stratocasters and other models of the moment."],
  ["L-prefix plate", "Late 1962 to late 1965", "A smaller plate with an L in front of five digits in a smaller typeface. The last of the pre-CBS plates."],
  ["Big-F plate", "Late 1965 to 1975", "A physically larger plate with the digits stamped above a bold stylized F. The CBS plate."],
];

const tocItems: [string, string][] = [
  ["#start", "Start Here"],
  ["#fast", "The Five Fastest Checks"],
  ["#years", "Jaguar Year By Year"],
  ["#headstock", "Why The Logo Won't Help"],
  ["#neck", "The Board And The Binding"],
  ["#guard", "The Pickguard"],
  ["#electronics", "Pickups And The Dual Circuit"],
  ["#bridge", "Bridge, Tremolo And Mute"],
  ["#heel", "The Neck Heel Date"],
  ["#body", "Body Dates"],
  ["#serial", "What The Serial Number Adds"],
  ["#finish", "Fullerplast And Refinishes"],
  ["#limits", "What Features Can't Prove"],
  ["#cross", "Confirm It From The Inside"],
  ["#faq", "Common Questions"],
  ["#appraisal", "Free Expert Appraisal"],
  ["#about-joe", "About Joe"],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Jaguar?",
    a: "Work it in three passes and expect each to give you a window rather than an answer. Start with the inlays and the fingerboard binding, because between them they cut the whole run into slices from across the room. Then read the pickguard, the tuners, and the serial number together, treating the serial as one vote among several. Then open it up for the parts Fender actually dated: the neck heel stamp and the body date, backed up by the pot codes. Those last two are the only things on the guitar that will give you a month. One warning specific to this model: do not start with the headstock. On a Jaguar the decal covers six years and will not tell you which side of CBS you are on.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why Doesn't The Logo Tell Me If My Jaguar Is Pre-CBS?",
    a: "Because the same decal ran straight through the changeover. The gold transition logo went on Jaguars from the 1962 launch until about the middle of 1968, so a 1963 and a 1967 wear the same logo and the decal by itself separates neither from the other. This is the opposite of a Stratocaster, where the logo is one of the fastest era checks on the guitar, and it is the single most common reason people misdate a Jaguar. What does work on the headstock is the patent block underneath the model name, because the number of patents changed by era. Everything else has to come from the board, the guard, the hardware, and the dates inside.",
  },
  {
    q: "Did The Jaguar Ever Have A Spaghetti Logo?",
    a: "No, never from the factory. The Jaguar arrived in 1962, after the spaghetti era had already ended on most Fender production, so it launched on the gold transition logo and never wore the thin script at all. A listing that describes a spaghetti logo on a Jaguar is either using the wrong name for the transition decal, which is a common slip even among experienced sellers, or showing a guitar wearing a decal that did not come on it. Worth checking rather than assuming, because a replaced decal raises questions about the whole neck.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Bound-Dot Jaguar?",
    a: "A factory build from the window between two changes. White fingerboard binding arrived around September 1965 but the pearloid block inlays did not follow until about the middle of 1966, so for those months Fender fitted bound boards that still carried pearloid dots. Bound-Dot necks are mostly 1966 production with a small tail into very late 1965, and they are correct rather than odd. The changeover to blocks was messy too, so a Bound-Dot neck dated a week or two after blocks officially arrived is still factory original. They are not rare, but they are a little less common than the longer running builds either side of them and some buyers seek the look out.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is My Jaguar Pre-CBS?",
    a: "Pre-CBS means built before CBS completed the purchase in January 1965, but the guitars did not change on that date and the Jaguar is the model where that matters most. The useful markers cluster later than the calendar: the guard goes to three ply during 1965, binding arrives around September 1965, and blocks arrive mid-1966. An unbound board with clay dots, a four ply nitrate guard, and black bottom pickups is comfortably pre-CBS. An early 1965 with those same features is a pre-CBS guitar in everything but the paperwork, and it is worth substantially more than a late 1965 that has the binding and the three ply guard. Look at the parts, not the year on the listing.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Jaguar Buzzes And The Strings Keep Popping Out Of The Saddles. Is Something Wrong With It?",
    a: "Almost certainly not. It is the design. The 24 inch scale means lower string tension at concert pitch than a Stratocaster's 25.5 inch, the bridge has a shallow break angle behind the saddles, and the factory saddle grooves are narrow and shallow. Fender expected the heavy flatwound strings that were standard in the early 60s. Put modern 9s or 10s on an unmodified vintage Jaguar and you get exactly the buzzing and popping you are describing. A heavier string set usually fixes it. So does a Mastery or Staytrem bridge, which drops into the original thimbles and pulls straight back out. Be careful with the other common fix though, because a Buzz Stop has to be drilled into the tremolo plate and those holes are permanent.",
  },
  {
    q: "How Do I Know If My Jaguar Pickups Are Original?",
    a: "Start with the bottoms, because that is the check serious buyers make first. Fender used black Forbon flatwork from 1962 to about the middle of 1964, then switched to gray for the rest of the run. Original black bottoms in a 1962 to early 1964 Jaguar are a collector premium and a clean pair is worth real money on its own. A guitar sold as a 1963 with gray bottom pickups is a replacement pair, a parts mix, or misdated, and none of those should carry full pre-CBS money. While the pickup is out, look at the pole pieces too: flat poles are correct on 1962 production, the change to staggered runs across 1963 and 1964 with mixed examples throughout, and flat poles on a 1965 or later guitar point at a swap.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I Take My Jaguar Apart To Date It?",
    a: "Only as far as you need to and only if you have done it before. Everything in the first pass reads with the guitar assembled, which is deliberate. Pulling a pickup to read the bottom color is worth it on an early guitar, and pulling the control plates to read pot codes is standard, but old nitrate pickguards are brittle and crack at the screw ears if you rush them. The Jaguar also has more small parts under the guard than any other Fender of the period, including the brass shielding and the two separate circuits, so it is an easy guitar to put back together slightly wrong. If the outside checks leave you stuck between two years, send me photos before you reach for a screwdriver.",
  },
];

// ---- Schema ----
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How To Date A Fender Jaguar, Year By Year",
  description:
    "Date a vintage Fender Jaguar from the features you can see: the inlays, the fingerboard binding, the pickguard, and the patent block, year by year from 1962 to 1975, and why the headstock logo will not help.",
